From 07c99cfc90a5158d04f8a53f1887583ad3f88160 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher AMIAUD <christopher.amiaud@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 13:10:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] Keep /proc up to date on emscripten

htop listed no processes, since it reads /proc/<pid>/task, and nothing
in /proc changed as processes ran: their cpu time, memory and state
were written once, and /proc/stat, /proc/loadavg and /proc/meminfo were
left to whoever embeds blink.

Opening anything under /proc now rewrites it first, at most five times
a second, unless a process was forked or ran a program meanwhile. The
cpu time of a process is the time its machine spends outside of system
calls, as tracked by OpSyscall(). /proc/stat shares it out between as
many cpus as the host has, which /sys/devices/system/cpu lists, since
htop counts them there. Load averages decay like the kernel's, and
sysinfo() reports the same uptime, load and memory, since busybox and
btop ask it rather than /proc. Zombies stay listed until reaped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
